2. BUNDLED THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL
================================================================================

vendor/astronomy.js
    astronomy-engine 2.1.19 by Don Cross <cosinekitty@gmail.com>
    https://github.com/cosinekitty/astronomy
    MIT License, Copyright (c) 2019-2023 Don Cross.
    Vendored unmodified; the full licence text is embedded at the top of the
    file and must stay there.

vendor/satellite.js
    satellite-js 5.0.0 by Shashwat Kandadai and UCSC
    https://github.com/shashwatak/satellite-js
    MIT License. The upstream copyright banner is the first six lines of the
    file and must stay there.

    Not vendored unmodified. Eight lines are appended, marked as an addition,
    re-exporting the UMD global so the file is a valid ES module: 5.0.0 is the
    last release with a single-file build, and this project has no bundler.
    Nothing above that block is changed.

data/coastline.json, data/borders.json, data/lakes.json
    Derived from Natural Earth's 1:50m coastline, admin-0 boundary lines and
    lakes
    https://www.naturalearthdata.com/
    Public domain. Natural Earth asks for no attribution and grants no
    warranty; this project credits it anyway, on the About page.

    None is a verbatim copy: tools/build_coastline.py and
    tools/build_overlays.py quantise every coordinate and re-encode it. Public
    domain source, so the derivatives carry no obligation, and they are covered
    by section 1 above as part of this repository.

data/transmitters.json
    Derived from SatNOGS DB, by the Libre Space Foundation
    https://db.satnogs.org/
    Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0)
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

    THIS FILE IS NOT MIT. It is the only bundled thing here carrying an
    obligation -- Natural Earth is public domain and the vendored libraries are
    MIT. SatNOGS DB is share-alike, so this file and any adaptation of it stay
    under CC BY-SA 4.0 and must be attributed. The attribution is on the About
    page and in the file's own "source" and "license" fields.

    Share-alike attaches to the data, not to the software that reads it:
    js/satellites.js and js/render.js remain MIT under section 1. Only this
    file, and anything derived from it, carries BY-SA.

    Generated by tools/build_transmitters.py, which selects transmitters marked
    alive and active for the nine satellites in ROSTER, keeps six fields, and
    caps each satellite at five entries. Frequencies are unchanged.

================================================================================
3. NOT BUNDLED
================================================================================

Space weather is read live from NOAA's Space Weather Prediction Center
(services.swpc.noaa.gov). Nothing from it is stored or redistributed here. As
a work of the United States government it is in the public domain, but this
project makes no claim over it and speaks for none of it.

Satellite element sets are read live from CelesTrak's amateur group
(celestrak.org). They are cached in the browser's local storage on the device
that fetched them, and are neither stored in this repository nor redistributed
by it. This project makes no claim over them and speaks for none of them.
